=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: mswitch1
diagnosis_text:
The fault lies on mswitch1, not router0. The VLAN 10 sub-interface eth0.10 on
mswitch1 is administratively/operationally DOWN (link flags show
<BROADCAST,MULTICAST> with no LOWER_UP, and `ip link` reports `state DOWN`),
while its parent physical interface eth0 is UP and the sibling sub-interface
eth0.20 is fully UP and forwarding.

mswitch1 acts as a multi-VLAN bridge. It bridges VLAN 10 traffic across three
sub-interfaces: eth0.10 (toward switch1, which carries PC4/PC5), eth2.10 (toward
switch2), and eth4.10 (toward router0). With eth0.10 down, the br_vlan10 bridge
has no path to switch1: no VLAN-10 frames from PC4 or PC5 can cross mswitch1 to
reach router0's eth0.10 gateway at 172.16.10.1. The FDB for br_vlan10 on eth0.10
contains zero dynamically learned MACs from the switch1 side, confirming that no
traffic has passed through it. The VLAN 20 path (eth0.20 → br_vlan20 → eth4.20)
remains fully intact, which is exactly why laptop0 on VLAN 20 is unaffected.

Router0's eth0.10 sub-interface is UP with the correct IP (172.16.10.1/24) and
is not the cause — the team's premise was incorrect.

Root cause: mswitch1's eth0.10 VLAN sub-interface has been brought down,
severing the VLAN 10 bridge path between switch1 and router0.

Corrective fix: On mswitch1, run `ip link set dev eth0.10 up`. This will
re-enable the sub-interface, restore the VLAN 10 bridge segment, and allow PC4
and PC5 to reach gateway 172.16.10.1 immediately.
=== END ===
